{#-
  The WordPress search form, reproduced from the export so the Author theme's
  existing `.search-form-container` rules style it with no additions — including
  the 70%/30% field-and-button split it applies inside `.main`.

  `method="get"` and `name="s"` are kept deliberately: this is a plain HTML form
  and needs no JavaScript to work. Submitting it lands on the search page, which
  reads the term back out of the URL.

  Expects `formId`. WordPress hardcoded `id="search-field"`, but the search page
  renders this partial twice — in the sidebar and again under the results — and
  two elements cannot share an id.
-#}
{%- set t = i18n[lang] -%}
<div class="search-form-container">
	<form role="search" method="get" class="search-form" action="{{ t.searchUrl }}">
		<label class="screen-reader-text" for="{{ formId }}">{{ t.search }}</label>
		<input id="{{ formId }}" type="search" class="search-field" value="" name="s" title="{{ t.searchFor }}">
		<input type="submit" class="search-submit" value="{{ t.searchGo }}">
	</form>
</div>
